GSP-RM RPC message-queue recorder — hooks for open-gpu-kernel-modules 575.51.03

Task #180, `multiarch-traces`. Spec: docs/design/rpc_trace_capture.md.

★ THIS IS `rpctrace.patch` RE-ANCHORED, NOT A SECOND RECORDER.

`nv_rpctrace.c` / `nv_rpctrace.h` are byte-identical between the two versions and
are not in either patch. Six of the eight hunks below are the 580 patch's hunks
verbatim; only the two in `GspMsgQueueReceiveStatus` are re-expressed, because
575 and 580 differ there STRUCTURALLY and a forced apply would have been a guess:

  ⚠ MEASURED, 2026-08-03: `patch -p1 --dry-run` of `rpctrace.patch` against a
    pristine 575.51.03 tree reports `2 out of 8 hunks FAILED` (#6 at :783 and
    #8 at :822). Both are in the receive path.

  #6 — 580's read-failure branch does `goto exit;`, reaching a shared `exit:`
       label that marks the queue consumed. 575 has NO `exit:` label and
       `return nvStatus;` directly. The accounting call is unchanged; it is
       placed before the `return` instead of before the `goto`.

  #8 — consequently 580's receive record sits immediately above `exit:`. In 575
       it sits immediately above the `if (nvStatus == NV_OK) { pMQI->rxSeqNum++;
       ... msgqRxMarkConsumed(...) }` block. ⊘ These are the same point in the
       DATA FLOW — after `ccslDecryptWithRotationChecks`, after the msgLen
       sanity check, before the element is released — which is the property the
       spec's §2.1 ordering trap actually constrains. `build_instrumented.sh`
       re-asserts it on the post-patch text (`send < encrypt`, `decrypt < recv`)
       rather than trusting this paragraph.

The recorded bytes, the record format and the three accounting calls are
identical to the 580 patch. Nothing about what is captured changes.

Apply with: patch -p1  from the root of an open-gpu-kernel-modules 575.51.03
checkout. `build_instrumented.sh --version 575.51.03` does it.
---
